# Real-objects formalization: boundary code feasibility audit (Phase 9 items 9e–9i)

**Phase 9 items 9e–9i (2026-04-24).** Determine whether
`BoundaryCodeContract` (item 8x) can be *constructed* from the
current `RewriteCalculusSetup` data, or whether it must remain an
*obligation* — and in either case, isolate the boundary side of the
residue admin quotient realization into the cleanest possible form.

## Audit of available data (item 9e)

`RewriteCalculusSetup` exposes:

* `BoundaryObject : Type u` — **completely opaque**. No
  `DecidableEq`, no `LinearOrder`, no serialization map, no
  `Repr` instance. The setup carries only the projection
  `boundaryOf : State → BoundaryObject`.
* `RefinedInterface : Type u` — **also completely opaque**. No
  decidable equality, no order, no serialization.
* `BoundaryAdminEquiv : BoundaryObject → BoundaryObject → Prop` —
  the free equivalence relation generated by `BoundaryTwoStepSwap`
  (defined in [SwapSquare.lean](Lean/TraceCalc/LayerB/RealObjects/SwapSquare.lean)).
  Its `ofTwoStepSwap` constructor is parameterized over an entire
  ambient record `R`, two indices `s, t`, and an `IndependentSinks`
  witness — there is no syntactic representative of an element of
  the quotient that does not refer back to such a generator term.
* `FrontierWord.BoundaryEquiv` (item 8v) splits as
  `Y_rel : BoundaryAdminEquiv … …` plus
  `externalOut_perm : List.Perm … …` over `RefinedInterface`.

**Conclusion (item 9e)**: with the current opaque `BoundaryObject`
and `RefinedInterface`, *no concrete canonical code* can be
constructed for either:

* `BoundaryAdminEquiv` modulo the boundary-admin quotient, or
* `List.Perm` on `List RefinedInterface`.

Both require *external* canonicalization data — at minimum a
serialization map for `BoundaryObject` (or a proof that
`BoundaryAdminEquiv` is equality on a chosen canonical
representative), and a key/serialization for `RefinedInterface` so
that lists can be sorted into a permutation-invariant form. The
right outcome of this batch is therefore an **obligation
interface**, not a construction.

## Items in this file

* **9e** — This audit (above).
* **9f** — `ExternalOutCodeContract setup`: a coding contract for
  `List.Perm` on `List setup.RefinedInterface`. Both `sound` and
  `complete` carry **real signatures** against `List.Perm`. Stated
  as an obligation; not instantiated.
* **9g** — `BoundaryAdminCodeContract setup`: a coding contract for
  `BoundaryAdminEquiv`. Both `sound` and `complete` carry **real
  signatures**. Stated as an obligation; not instantiated.
* **9h** — `boundary_component_codes_to_contract`: assembling
  `BoundaryAdminCodeContract` and `ExternalOutCodeContract` into a
  `BoundaryCodeContract` for `FrontierWord.BoundaryEquiv` (item
  8x). The product target is `BoundaryCode × ExternalOutCode`; the
  sound/complete proofs follow the same `Prod.mk.injEq` /
  `congrArg Prod.fst/snd` pattern as the interior assembly (item
  8y).
* **9i** — Manuscript-facing aliases and explicit stop report.

## Honest scope (per user's stop conditions, all honored)

* No concrete serialization for `setup.BoundaryObject` is invented.
* `BoundaryAdminEquiv` is **not** assumed to collapse to equality.
* `FrontierQuotientRealization` is **not** claimed: even with
  `interiorCanonicalContract` constructed, the assembly theorem
  `component_codes_to_quotient_realization` (item 8y) requires a
  `BoundaryCodeContract` instance, which is not supplied here —
  only the *obligation interface* is.
* `FrontierWord` is **not** enriched.
* `BoundaryObject` is **not** enriched (no `DecidableEq` / `Repr`
  instance is added to `RewriteCalculusSetup`).

## Stop report (item 9i)

The current obstacle to a fully-constructed
`FrontierQuotientRealization` is **not** Lean bookkeeping. It is
the *absence of a canonical serialization or quotient code* for
`BoundaryAdminEquiv` and `List.Perm`-on-`RefinedInterface`. After
this batch, the entire residue-level holographic theorem is
reduced to two named boundary-canonicalization theorems:

* `BoundaryAdminCodeContract` (item 9g), and
* `ExternalOutCodeContract` (item 9f).

These two together yield `BoundaryCodeContract` via item 9h, which
together with `interiorCanonicalContract` (item 9d) yields the
faithful `FrontierQuotientRealization` via item 8y.

